## 5️⃣ STP FRAMEWORK ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ (MOST IMPORTANT — highest PYQ weight in this chapter)

### The Flow

```
   SEGMENTATION          TARGETING            POSITIONING
   "Group buyers by  →   "Pick the BEST   →   "Own a specific
    similar needs"        group to serve"      image in their mind"
```

---

### A. Segmentation
> **Definition:** Dividing customers into groups having **similar needs and wants**.

**Business Scenario:** A campus food stall studies student food preferences and finds four natural groups: North Indian, South Indian, Chinese, Continental. These four groups are the **segments**.

> 🧠 **Memory Trick:** Segmentation = **Grouping Customers**.

---

### B. Targeting
> **Definition:** Selecting the **best (most attractive) segment** to serve.

**Worked Example:**

| Food Segment | % of Students Preferring |
|---|---|
| North Indian | 25% |
| **South Indian** | **35%** ⭐ |
| Chinese | 20% |
| Continental | 20% |

**Decision:** Highest percentage → **South Indian** → this becomes the **target segment**.

> 🧠 **Memory Trick:** Targeting = **Choose the Best Group** (based on size, profitability, or fit).

> ⚠️ **Exam Tip:** "Best" doesn't always mean "largest" in real business — sometimes it means most profitable or most strategically aligned. But for exam numerical questions based on survey %, default to the **highest percentage** unless told otherwise.

---

### C. Positioning
> **Definition:** Creating a **positive image** of the product in the customer's mind.

**Example statement:** *"Our food is healthy, nutritious, and affordable."*

> 🧠 **Memory Trick:** Positioning = **Customer's Perception** (what they think of you, not just what you sell).

---

### 🎯 STP Decision Framework (apply this to any case study)

```
STEP 1: SEGMENT the market
   → Ask: "What different groups exist with different needs?"

STEP 2: TARGET the best segment
   → Ask: "Which group is most attractive (biggest/most profitable/easiest to serve)?"

STEP 3: POSITION your offering
   → Ask: "What one clear idea do I want THIS segment to associate with my brand?"
```

> 💼 **Business Insight:** STP is why Maruti Suzuki sells both the Alto (budget segment) and the Nexa lineup (premium segment) — same company, different segments targeted, and each positioned completely differently in the customer's mind.

