## 8️⃣ COST PRICE (CP), SELLING PRICE (SP) & PROFIT/LOSS ⭐⭐⭐⭐

| Term | Definition | Example |
|---|---|---|
| **Cost Price (CP)** | Cost incurred in making the product | Pen manufacturing cost = ₹10 |
| **Selling Price (SP)** | Price at which the product is actually sold | Pen sold for ₹25 |

### 🔀 The Three Outcomes

```
   SP > CP   →   ✅ PROFIT     (e.g., CP=₹10, SP=₹15 → Profit=₹5)
   SP = CP   →   ⚖️ NO PROFIT, NO LOSS  (e.g., CP=₹10, SP=₹10)
   SP < CP   →   ❌ LOSS       (e.g., CP=₹10, SP=₹8 → Loss=₹2)
```

| Condition | Result |
|---|---|
| SP > CP | Profit |
| SP = CP | No Profit, No Loss (break-even) |
| SP < CP | Loss |

---

### 💼 Business Scenario: Why sell BELOW Cost Price?

Sometimes companies deliberately sell at a loss to **minimize further losses**, rather than not selling at all.

**Examples:**
- Clearing old mobile phone models before a new launch
- Liquidating unsold/perishable inventory

**Logic:** Selling a ₹10-cost item for ₹8 (a ₹2 loss) is still better than not selling it at all and eventually writing off the full ₹10 as a total loss.

> ⚠️ **Exam Tip:** This "sell below cost to reduce loss" logic is a favorite scenario-based question. The key phrase to write in your answer: *"Partial recovery of cost is better than total write-off."*

